# Network Battleship

Network Battleship is a two-player command-line Battleship game played over a network. It follows the basic rules of Battleship but adds hidden power-ups that introduce additional strategy.

## Game Rules

### Player Roles
- Two players connect to the game over the network.
- Each player has their own grid.
- Players place their ships before the game begins.
- Players also secretly place power-ups around their own grid.
- Neither player can see the opponent's ship or power-up locations.

### Turns
- Players alternate turns.
- During a normal turn, a player selects a coordinate on the opponent's grid to attack.
- The game reports whether the attack was a hit, miss, or discovered power-up.
- If a player discovers a power-up, it is added to their inventory.
- Power-ups can be saved and used on a later turn.

### Turn Costs and Power-Up Use
- A player may activate at most one power-up per turn.
- A normal attack targets one square and ends the turn.
- Nuke replaces the normal attack with a plus-sign attack and ends
  the turn.
- Radar replaces the normal attack with a scan and ends the turn.
- Shield replaces the normal attack by protecting one of the player's
  own unhit ship squares and ends the turn.
- Extra Shot replaces the normal attack with two attacks at different
  coordinates. The turn ends after the second attack.
- EMP does not consume the normal attack. After activating EMP, the
  player makes one normal attack, and the opponent skips their next
  turn. The player cannot activate another power-up that turn.
- If an attack destroys the opponent's final remaining ship square,
  the game ends immediately, even if an extra attack remains.

### Targeting Rules
- Invalid actions do not consume a turn or a power-up.
- Normal attacks cannot target a square already resolved as a hit,
  miss, or collected power-up.
- A blocked attack consumes the Shield but leaves the ship square
  undamaged. That square may be attacked again on a later turn.
- Nuke targets a center square and its four immediate neighbors.
  Squares outside the board or already resolved are ignored.
  At least one target square must remain attackable.
- Each power-up square hit by Nuke is collected normally.
- Radar uses a center coordinate whose full 3 × 3 area must fit
  inside the board. Its center row and column must therefore be 1–6.
- Radar reports the number of undestroyed enemy ship squares in
  that area, including shielded squares, without revealing locations.
- Shield may only target an unhit, unshielded square of the player's
  own ship.
- Extra Shot attacks are processed separately. An invalid second
  target may be retried without losing the remaining attack.

## Hidden Power-Ups

Power-ups are placed around each player's own board before the game starts. However, the power-ups are actually rewards for the opponent.

If the opponent attacks a square containing a power-up, they acquire that power-up and can save it for later use.

### Nuke
- The Nuke attacks five squares in a plus-sign pattern.
- The player chooses the center coordinate.
- The center, left, right, above, and below squares are attacked.

Example:

      X
    X X X
      X

### EMP
- The EMP disables the opponent for one turn.
- The EMP can be activated without using the player's normal attack.
- After activating the EMP, the player can still attack normally during the same turn.
- The opponent then skips their next turn.

### Radar
- The player selects a 3x3 area of the opponent's board.
- The game reports how many ship squares are located within that area.
- The exact ship locations are not revealed.

### Shield
- The Shield can protect one ship square from an incoming attack.
- If the protected square is attacked, the attack is blocked.
- The Shield is removed after blocking one attack.

### Extra Shot
- The Extra Shot allows the player to make two attacks instead of one during a turn.

## Power-Up Inventory

On a later turn, the player may use a stored power-up according to
the Turn Costs and Power-Up Use rules above.

When a player discovers a power-up, it is stored in their inventory. On a later turn, the player can choose between making a normal attack or using one of their available power-ups.

Example:

    Your Turn

    1. Fire
    2. Use Power-Up

    Power-Up Inventory:
    - Nuke
    - Radar

## Victory Condition

A player wins when all of the opponent's ships have been completely destroyed.

Power-ups do not need to be discovered or destroyed to win the game.

## Draw / Tie Condition

Under normal gameplay, there is no draw because players continue alternating turns until one player's entire fleet has been destroyed.

## Networking

The game will use a client/server architecture to allow two players to play from separate machines over a network.

The server will maintain the game state and communicate player actions between the two clients.


## DNS Configuration

The authoritative DNS domain name for the game server will be:

    server.shakin.edu

Clients will use this domain name to locate and connect to the Battleship game server.

### Board and Fleet
- Each player has an 8 × 8 grid.
- Row and column coordinates range from 0 to 7.
- Each player places three ships: one 2-square ship, one 3-square
  ship, and one 4-square ship.
- Ships must be placed horizontally or vertically.
- Ships cannot overlap or extend outside the grid.
- Each fleet occupies 9 squares total.
- A player wins by destroying all 9 of the opponent's ship squares.

### Power-Up Placement
- Each player places five hidden power-ups: one Nuke, one EMP,
  one Radar, one Shield, and one Extra Shot.
- Each power-up occupies one square.
- Power-ups cannot overlap ships or other power-ups.
- All placements must be within the 8 × 8 grid.
- When an opponent attacks a power-up square, the opponent collects
  it and the power-up is removed from the board.
- Each collected power-up can be used only once and cannot be used
  during the same turn it was collected.